  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a factor of safety?",
      "a": "The ratio of the strength of a part to the stress it actually carries. A factor of 2.5 means the part could take two and a half times its working stress before failing.",
      "asks": [
        "factor of safety formula"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are loads and strengths shown as spreads?",
      "a": "Real loads vary from day to day and real materials vary from batch to batch. Each is a spread of values, and failure happens where a high load meets a weak part.",
      "asks": [
        "stress strength interference"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I choose a factor of safety?",
      "a": "Use a bigger factor when loads or materials are uncertain or failure would be dangerous, and a smaller one when everything is well tested and weight matters. Design codes give values for many cases.",
      "asks": [
        "typical factor of safety values"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I use a factor of safety to size a part?",
      "a": "Divide the material strength by the factor to get the allowable stress, then divide the load by that stress to get the area you need.",
      "asks": [
        "allowable stress"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Which strength should I use, yield or ultimate?",
      "a": "For ductile metals use the yield strength, because permanent bending already counts as failure. For brittle materials, which do not yield, use the ultimate strength.",
      "asks": [
        "yield vs ultimate for safety factor"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Aircraft structures are commonly designed with a factor of safety of about 1.5 on limit loads.",
    "Factor of safety = strength ÷ working stress."
